Part of the Perl CLI strand (see the strand card).

A differential test, t/54-parity-cli.t. It runs both CLIs on one machine, as t/52 does, over the same case format, and covers what golden files cannot pin:
- git failure wording;
- the real host and user in templates;
- the state files (sources-read and unreached, after placeholders; render-inputs, whose hash covers absolute paths);
- both CLIs sharing one sync lock.

The test skips without node or dist/skilletor.js, with a named reason.

2026-11-14 00:55 Progress: perl/t/54-parity-cli.t + new helper perl/t/lib/SkilletorTest/ParityCLI.pm written; green in the shared tree and on a HEAD worktree (~48 s alone). Design (mine): each machine is set up once, saved, and restored before each CLI's run, so both CLIs run in the very same paths - every byte compares raw, no path placeholders, render-inputs hash included. Parts: (1) five conformance cases through both CLIs (24, 32, 52, 60, 64: state the golden trees leave out; engine ops run as their command --json); (2) own machine: git failure wording (missing repo, missing ref, cache reuse, ls-remote in check, no git on PATH -> spawn git ENOENT, a git that fails silently -> Command failed), real host/user/project in a .njk, render-inputs/sources-read/unreached/last-check/session-cwd; (3) one sync lock: a holder whose fetch is slowed through ext::, the other CLI's sync + SessionStart wait (2 s) or time out (8 s), both directions. No difference found so far. Next: mutations, skip checks, suite timing.

